FILES_READ:
src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py: 131

SUSPICIONS:
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::DXCLICompleter.get_matches", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C8.S4", "lines": [286, 287], "why": "'dxpy.find_apps(describe=...)' lists every app with its installed flag over the API on each call. get_matches runs on every TAB press (complete() calls it when state is 0) for run, install and uninstall. Nothing is stored on the completer, so the same listing is fetched again on each press."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::rm", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C8.S4", "lines": [899, 919], "why": "With -r and without -f, the loop at 895-913 calls resolve_existing_path(path, allow_mult=True, all_mult=args.all) for each path only to test for the whole-project case, then discards the results. The loop at 916 calls the same function with the same arguments for the same paths, so each path costs two sets of API lookups. The first-pass results could be kept and reused."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::describe", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C8.S4", "lines": [1321, 1322, 1323], "why": "For an analysis ID the entity lookup at 1257 already passes defaultFields (set at 1246, forced by spotCostSavings at 1240), and that describe is stored in result['describe']. Under --verbose, line 1322 calls dxpy.DXAnalysis(result['id']).describe() with no fields, which fetches the default fields a second time. The update at 1323 appears to add nothing the first call did not return. Confidence is medium: I checked the request inputs but did not compare server responses."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::cd", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C1.S6", "lines": [737, 740], "why": "'project_name = try_call(dxpy.get_handler(project).describe)['name']' makes an API describe on every cd. The name is used only inside the branch guarded at 740. When project equals dxpy.WORKSPACE_ID and DX_PROJECT_CONTEXT_NAME is already in os.environ, that branch is skipped and the describe result is thrown away. The case is narrow because it needs the variable exported, but the call runs unconditionally."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::find_executions", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C5.S1", "lines": [2433, 2434, 2445, 2447], "why": "executions_by_parent[parent] is a list (defaultdict(list), line 2405). Each new child runs 'execution_id.id not in executions_by_parent[parent]' at 2433, a linear scan, and the stage loop at 2445 does the same scan. For a parent with many children (scatter jobs, large analyses), the work grows with the square of the child count. A set kept beside each list would make the membership test constant time, while the list keeps the order that build_tree iterates. This overlaps C3.S4."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::get_database", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C1.S5", "lines": [1951, 1952, 1955, 1960, 1965, 1975], "why": "Each do_debug call formats its message eagerly with str.format. That includes repr of args and the database entity, and at line 1975 the whole list_folder_resp, which can hold one entry per database file. The module sets logging.basicConfig(level=INFO) at line 26, so these strings are dropped by default, yet they are built on every call. Lazy logging arguments, or a guard on the logger level, would skip the work."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::run_body", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C6.S2", "lines": [3234], "why": "'copy.deepcopy(args.cloned_job_desc).get(\"systemRequirements\", {})' deep copies the whole cloned job description, including runInput, output and details, to read one key. The copy is only read afterward, by SystemRequirementsDict.from_sys_requirements at 3235-3239, and SystemRequirementsDict.__init__ already deep copies its entrypoints (system_requirements.py). Reading args.cloned_job_desc.get('systemRequirements', {}) directly gives the same values."}, {"label": "b", "root_cause": "C8.S4", "lines": [3272, 3375], "why": "Each of these lines describes the same executable again. ExecutableInputs.get_executable_description (cli/exec_io.py line 489) already calls executable.describe() at the top of run_body, through _get_input_for_run at line 3225, and DXApp.describe does not cache. Line 3375 reuses the 3272 result when 3272 ran, so at most one extra call happens: at 3272 with --instance-count, or at 3375 with --priority low or normal."}, {"label": "c", "root_cause": "C8.S4", "lines": [3389], "why": "For every workflow stage, dxpy.describe(stage_desc['executable']) fetches the executable again with no memo. A workflow that uses the same app or applet in several stages repeats the same describe. This runs only on the low or normal priority path for workflows, and the saving depends on how often stages share an executable, so confidence is moderate."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::generate_batch_inputs", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C1.S4", "lines": [4210], "why": "Inside flatten_batch, 'sorted(b['inputs'].items())' appears twice in one return expression, so each batch row sorts the same dict twice. One local computed per row would serve both list comprehensions. The saving is small, since each row has only a few inputs."}]}
{"unit": "src/python/dxpy/scripts/dx.py::archive", "suspicions": [{"label": "a", "root_cause": "C9.S1", "lines": [4374, 4381, 4383], "why": "For each name in possible_files, the loop calls dxpy.find_data_objects(classname='file', name=..., folder=..., describe=True, recurse=False) once, which is one API round trip per file. The help text allows up to 1000 files per request, and files that share a folder could be answered by one listing of that folder, filtered by name. Confidence is moderate because I did not check whether the API offers a batch name lookup."}]}

NOTES:
- All 6914 lines of dx.py were read. The 131 units are 126 top-level functions and class methods plus 5 methods of the ExecutionId class nested in find_executions, which I counted as methods. Nested helper functions were treated as part of their enclosing unit.
- Module-level code (imports, and the parser construction from about line 4614 to 6879) is not a unit. I read it in full and found no energy finding there.
- Decisions rest on the README index lines and the code itself. I did not open the cards under reference/categories or reference/root-causes, so my root-cause labels may not match the card wording.
- Helper files read to check claims: system_requirements.py, cli/exec_io.py, cli/parsers.py, utils/resolver.py, utils/executable_unbuilder.py, bindings/dxapp.py. Nothing was run and nothing was measured. Two read-only counting scripts were written under /tmp, outside the repo.
- Left out as negligible or not decidable: payload['try'] reassigned inside the loops of add_tags, remove_tags, set_properties and unset_properties; headers rebuilt per batch file in generate_batch_inputs; len(list(roots.keys())) in find_executions; the project_describe access probe at line 1227 (its result is discarded, but it decides the fallback, so removing it would change behaviour); fixed 1 Hz polling in ssh and wait.
- Correctness problems seen while reading (not energy, not run): archive line 4381 builds the path from the stale variable obj instead of fp. upload_one re-runs process_dataobject_args on each child copy, and process_properties_args then iterates a dict it already produced, so a recursive upload with --properties should raise DXParserError (from reading only). wait line 2718 applies % to a string with {} placeholders, which raises TypeError. wait line 2715 leaves a file handle unclosed; CPython closes it at once, so I did not record it.

